# What never enters the Docker build context (FR-A-164). The image holds no secrets, no .env, no
# .git, no test files and no development dependencies: the build copies only what it names, and
# this file keeps the rest out of the context altogether, which is also faster and safer.
#
# Docker matches a pattern against the path from the context root, so a bare name only matches at
# the root: "**/" is what reaches the node_modules folders of the workspace packages. The last
# matching pattern wins, which is why the e2e exception comes after the e2e line.
# `apps/server/test/deploy-files.test.ts` evaluates this file with the same rules.

# Version control and the machine of the developer
.git
.githooks
.github
.worktrees
.idea
.vscode
.DS_Store
**/__pycache__

# Agent state, which may hold credentials
.claude
.copilot
.codex
.mcp.json

# Secrets and key material. Only .env.example is tracked, and the image has no use for it either.
**/.env*
**/*.pem
**/*.key
**/*.pfx
**/*.p12

# Runtime data of a local run (the /data volume is never part of an image)
data
**/*.sqlite
**/*.sqlite-wal
**/*.sqlite-shm

# Dependencies and build output: the build installs and builds inside the image
**/node_modules
**/dist
**/*.tsbuildinfo
**/.vite
**/coverage

# Test output
**/playwright-report
**/test-results
**/blob-report

# Not part of the image
docs
specs

# The end-to-end tests are not built into the image. The workspace lists the package, so the
# frozen lockfile install needs its manifest and nothing else.
e2e
!e2e/package.json

# VERSION is deliberately not listed: the build reads it (the Dockerfile checks the VERSION build
# argument against it), and so does the version check of the server build.
